        <div id="runtime-math" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Battery Runtime Math: Why It Is Hours, Not Nights
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the part most marketing skips. One watt equals about 3.41 BTU per hour, and household air conditioners typically produce somewhere around 10 to 12 BTU per hour for every watt they draw. That means a 5,000 BTU air conditioner running flat out draws in the region of 400 to 500 watts, and a 6,000 BTU unit somewhat more. Inverter compressors and eco modes reduce that once the space is cool, but the starting point is several hundred watts.
          </p>

          <p>
            Now divide battery capacity by draw. A 1,000 watt-hour battery feeding a 450 watt load lasts a little over two hours, before inverter losses. Running the AC through a power station AC outlet loses perhaps 10 to 15 percent more. Even a 2,000 watt-hour station gives roughly four hours of full-output cooling. Lower settings, a cool night, a shaded and insulated tent and an AC that cycles down can stretch that a good deal, which is where manufacturer claims of longer runtimes come from. A full eight-hour night of strong cooling on a single portable battery is not realistic.
          </p>

          <p>
            That does not make battery air conditioning useless. It makes it a tool for specific jobs: cooling the tent for the first few hours while you fall asleep, cooling a van during the hottest part of the afternoon, keeping a pet cool in a parked vehicle for a short stop, or cooling a sleeping area at a site with a generator curfew. Pre-cool the space on shore power or while driving, then let the battery hold the temperature rather than pull it down from scratch.
          </p>

          <p>
            Solar helps, but only so much. A 400W portable array in good midday sun might produce roughly 250 to 300 watts, which covers part of an AC draw while the sun is high and nothing at night, when most people want to sleep cool. For daytime cooling in a van with a large rooftop array and a big lithium bank, solar can cover a meaningful share. For a tent, it mostly refills the battery for the next evening. Our <a href="/best-solar-generator/">solar generator guide</a> and the <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">main power station roundup</a> cover the batteries and panels that pair best with these units.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Rough Full-Output Battery Runtime</p>
