        <div id="which-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Which Portable Air Conditioner Should You Buy?
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you camp at sites without power, want a real air conditioner and accept that battery runtime is measured in hours, buy the <strong>EcoFlow Wave 3</strong>. It has the most cooling of the battery units, heats as well, and works with the widest range of power sources. If it is over budget, or your tent or van is small, the <strong>EcoFlow Wave 2</strong> delivers most of the same experience for less, especially when discounted. Either one pairs naturally with an EcoFlow power station; our <a href="/best-ecoflow-power-station/">EcoFlow lineup guide</a> covers which models match.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you mainly need to cool a small tent while you sleep, or a car or truck cab for short periods, the <strong>Zero Breeze Mark 3</strong> is the most portable option and its lower draw stretches battery runtime. Point it at the sleeping area, shade the tent, and it can make a hot night comfortable. Just do not expect it to cool a big family tent in the afternoon.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your campsites have electric hookups, skip the battery units entirely. The <strong>Midea Duo 12,000</strong> cools large tents, cabins and RV living areas far better for the money. If your tent has an AC port or you camp in a tent trailer, the <strong>Midea U 8,000</strong> window unit is quieter and more efficient. For the tightest budget and a small tent on hookup or a 2,000-watt generator, the <strong>BLACK+DECKER 5,000 BTU</strong> window unit does the job for very little.
          </p>

          <p>
            And if the nights are dry and mild, consider skipping air conditioning altogether. A good battery fan costs a fraction of any unit here, runs all night on a small battery, and in the right climate is enough. Whatever you buy, start with shade, a reflective cover and a well-sealed hose port, and size the battery from our <a href="/best-portable-power-station-for-camping/">camping power station guide</a>, because the battery decides how long any of these can run.
          </p>
