        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying an evaporative cooler expecting air conditioning</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Swamp coolers and personal air coolers add moisture to the air and only cool noticeably in dry climates. In a humid campground or closed tent they barely beat a fan. If a product has no exhaust hose or outdoor section, it cannot remove heat from the space and is not an air conditioner.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Believing all-night runtime claims from a small battery</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 5,000 BTU air conditioner at full output draws roughly 400 to 500 watts, so a 1kWh battery lasts about two hours. Longer claims depend on eco modes, mild conditions and a cool, insulated space. Plan battery cooling for a few hours, and use hookups or a generator for the rest.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the exhaust hose inside the tent</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A portable AC moves heat from one place to another. If the hot exhaust stays inside the tent or van, the unit heats the space nearly as fast as it cools it. Route every hose outside through a window or port, seal the gap, and keep the hose as short and straight as possible.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pitching the tent in full sun</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A tent in direct sun absorbs a huge amount of heat through its fabric, and no portable AC can keep up for long. Pitch in shade, add a reflective tarp over the fly with an air gap, and cover mesh panels once the AC is on. Shade often matters more than extra BTU.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running the AC from the car 12V socket</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most vehicle accessory sockets are fused for roughly 10 to 15 amps, around 120 to 180 watts, which is far below what a real air conditioner draws. Plugging one in will blow the fuse or limit output badly. Use a power station, a properly wired house battery, or shore power instead.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a battery-powered air conditioner cool a tent all night?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not at full output on a typical portable battery. A 5,000 to 6,000 BTU unit draws several hundred watts, so a 1kWh battery lasts roughly two hours of strong cooling. With eco modes, cool night air, shade and insulation you can stretch it considerably, but reliable all-night cooling usually needs a hookup, a generator or a very large battery bank with solar.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many BTU do I need to cool a tent?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Tents need far more cooling per square foot than houses because they are poorly insulated and absorb sun. As a starting point, use 2,000 to 3,000 BTU for spot cooling a small tent, about 5,000 BTU for a shaded two- to four-person tent, and 6,000 to 8,000 BTU or more for a large family tent. Shade and a reflective cover reduce what you need.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is an evaporative swamp cooler the same as an air conditioner?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. An evaporative cooler blows air over a wet pad and cools it by evaporation, which adds humidity and only works well in dry climates. A real air conditioner uses a compressor and refrigerant to remove heat and moisture and vent it outside through a hose or window body. In humid weather, only a real AC makes a big difference.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I run a portable air conditioner from a power station?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if the station AC output exceeds the unit running and surge draw. A 1kWh station runs a 5,000 BTU unit for roughly two hours at strong output, a 2kWh station roughly four. Some camping units, like the EcoFlow Wave models, also connect to dedicated add-on batteries or EcoFlow power stations, which avoids some inverter losses.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is there a good 12V air conditioner for a car or truck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Permanently installed 12V and 24V cab air conditioners exist for trucks and vans, but they draw roughly 40 amps or more at 12V and need a large battery bank and heavy wiring. For portable use, a compact battery unit or a power station with an EcoFlow Wave is more practical. A car accessory socket cannot supply enough power.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size generator runs a camping air conditioner?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 2,000-watt-class inverter generator runs many 5,000 BTU window units and often an 8,000 BTU inverter unit, provided the running watts fit and the generator handles the compressor start-up surge. A soft-start device helps. Larger portable units like a 12,000 BTU model may need a 3,000-watt-class generator. Check campground quiet hours before relying on one.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do portable air conditioners need to be vented in a tent?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Every real air conditioner must send the heat it removes outside, or it simply moves heat around inside the tent. Route the exhaust hose through a window or AC port and seal the gap. Dual-hose designs work better in tents because they do not pull warm outside air in through the gaps.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

