          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            A real air conditioner at camp is possible, but the power budget decides everything. On battery, the EcoFlow Wave 3 and Wave 2 give you a few hours of genuine cooling, and the Zero Breeze Mark 3 trades output for longer runtime in small spaces. On a hookup or a generator, an inexpensive household unit like the Midea or BLACK+DECKER cools better for less.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Start with shade, a reflective cover and a sealed hose port, size the BTU to the space, and be honest with yourself about runtime. Do that and a hot-weather camping trip stops being an endurance test.
